identity-coordinate-transformation-b9c16.conceptual_definition:
The identity coordinate transformation, expressed as $x' = x$, is the simplest case of a coordinate transformation where the new coordinate system is identical to the old one. In the more general context of spacetime transformations $x'^{\mu} = f(x^{
u})$, the identity transformation corresponds to the function $f$ being the identity function, i.e., $f(x^{
u}) = x^{
u}$. It serves as the identity element in the group of coordinate transformations.

identity-coordinate-transformation-b9c16.interpretation:
Physically, the equation $x' = x$ signifies that the coordinate system used by an observer remains unchanged. This is the trivial case of a transformation. In the framework of diffeomorphism invariance, which states that the laws of physics are independent of the choice of coordinate system, the identity transformation is the baseline case. Any physical law expressed in terms of coordinates $x$ will have the exact same form when expressed in terms of $x'$, because $x'$ and $x$ are identical. This transformation is a specific instance of a general coordinate transformation $x'^{\mu} = f(x^{
u})$ where the Jacobian matrix of the transformation is the identity matrix, $ rac{\partial x'^{\mu}}{\partial x^{
u}} = \delta^{\mu}_{
u}$.
